<comment datatype='string'>&lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CoM&quot;&gt;Coenzyme M&lt;/a&gt; is a small molecule consisting of sulfhydryl and sulfonate functional groups linked by an ethane moiety. It functions as a methyl carrier in methanogenic archaea, where it serves as the terminal intermediate of methanogenesis from all substrates [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/2115763&quot;&gt;DiMarco90&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
In addition to its role in methanogenesis, &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CoM&quot;&gt;coenzyme M&lt;/a&gt; is used by some bacteria for the utilization of aliphtic alkenes, serving during ring opening of toxic epoxide intermediates [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/10411892&quot;&gt;Allen99&lt;/a&gt;].</comment>
